Once you have a GPU-ready solver, it makes sense to doublecheck quickly if the
solver really dispatches the correct compute kernels.
For this, we first study the generated fused-kernel call.
Stateless routines are also very useful on the CPU, as we can aggressively
vectorise over them.
Therefore, the generated code should expose a call similar to


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
_fused_compute_kernel_call = exahype2.solvers.fv.godunov.kernels.create_fused_compute_kernel_call(...)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The kernels might look completely different, but the principle is clear:
Whenever we prepare a fused batch, the generated call has to dispatch the
stateless variant that can run on the selected target.


## Fused compute kernels

@todo This will change completely as we switch to xDSL. Notably, we will not support
